


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: smith
@title: Music Encoding and the Monumental Edition
@presenter: smith
@abstract: 

Recent developments in the encoding of music notation have
led to exciting projects which employ computational
musicology to address specific research questions, and to
scholarly editions in which primary sources are transcribed
as faithfully as possible, preserving the intangible
cultural heritage within them in a readily accessible form.
However, the full potential of such digital possibilities
has yet to be realised in critical editing of the kind
found in European monumental editions. 

This paper uses early English keyboard music from the
sixteenth and seventeenth centuries as a case study because
in this repertoire there is no fixity of text leading to a
great deal of textual variance between sources. It builds
upon an AHRC-funded project, Digital Directions for
Collected Editions (2021–2023), in which questions around
the users of such editions were raised. It will explore the
differing needs of hypothetical users of such an edition
and propose ways in which these might be met in the digital
monumental edition of the future. 

Such an edition could cater on the same platform for both
‘best text’ approaches to editing and those in which texts
are conflated. It could be constructed in ‘layers’:
diplomatic editions of sources, source editions with the
minimum number of emendations made in reference to
concordant texts, and critical editions. Sources could be
viewed in parallel, and/or variants appear alongside a main
text. Users could choose between various aspects of
notation – for example, preservation of original beaming of
smaller note values in source editions or its
modernisation. Scholarly, critical editions could also
serve as the basis for practical, performing editions with
different audiences in mind – for example, one might
contain suggested ‘original’ fingerings for the advanced
student, whereas another might have modern fingerings for a
young pianist. The editor of such an edition could act also
as curator, guiding different categories of user to the
material that they need. Information on performance
practice, instruments, and the history of early English
keyboard music could be updated in the light of changing
scholarship, and unlike paper-based editions, the texts
themselves could be corrected if necessary without having
to publish a second edition.

The problems faced by the editor of digital editions are
the same as for any printed one. However, the encoding of
notation and a digital platform may offer easier (although
not necessarily cheaper) solutions.

@@END: PRESENTATION



